A||N||6||mope||-= Clutch pedal all aluminum, or just sorta? info please||||4||
Z||000000||mope||05-06-2001||11:37 PM||||My clutch is doing ok now that i got a new slave but it still isn't perfect. I heard that the original aluminum clutch pedals would bend. So i check my pedal with a magnet and it sticks. So its steel right? But then i read that the pedal was a blend or something, so would my magnet still stick to a blend of steel and aluminum? or is just part of it steel and i stuck my magnet in the wrong place? Also does anyone have a good pickture of a good pedal next to a bad one? mine looks twisted or something, but all the other ones ive seen look the same.<P>Any help on this topic would be alot o help!<BR>thanks<P>mope<P>------------------<BR>=======================<P>-= Silver 86 GT, being restored, and following major things to be done: Mr. Mike's Leather, new steering wheel, paint repair, ebrake repair, take apart and clean everything! <A HREF="http://www.mastergraphics.cjb.net" TARGET=_blank>www.mastergraphics.cjb.net</A>  <A HREF="http://www.mope.cjb.net" TARGET=_blank>www.mope.cjb.net</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||05-07-2001||04:17 AM||||As I remember, the aluminum clutch pedals were only used in early Fieros. Depending on when in 86 your Fiero was built it might have been one of the first to have a steel pedal. <P>To check involves taking out the pedal. We used to check by putting a straight edge like a pencil or the like throught the pivot bore. Looking straight down at the pedal the pencil and the rod where the cylinder piston hooks to should be parallel.<P>Hope that helps. ||||reg||6
Z||000002||Archie||05-07-2001||07:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mope:<BR><B> Also does anyone have a good pickture of a good pedal next to a bad one? <BR>mope<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just put up such pictures on this other active thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010194.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010194.html</A> <P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000003||mope||05-07-2001||05:21 PM||||my pedal is painted two tone black and silver, does that tell anyone anything?<P>So would my magnet stick to this blend pedal?<P>Im kinda lazy and dont wanna take the pedal out if i dont hafta.<P>thanks guys<P>Mope||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||05-07-2001||05:54 PM||||Mr. Magnet is <B>strongly</B> attracted to the pedal arm (the part that's just above the pad) on my 1987 GT, and 1988 Coupe.<P>Mr. Magnet is not attracted at <B>all</B> to the pedal arm on my 1986 SE.  So I am quite certain it's aluminum.  <P>The difference is very obvious to me; of course, I'm lucky enough to be able to compare the 2 types of pedal.  But, the part of the pedal we're talking about is the easiest part to get to - it's the "arm" that comes down and attaches to the pad (where your foot goes).  Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.     <P>     ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mope||05-07-2001||08:38 PM||||Thank you very much everyone, i have now determined that my pedal is steel, i still might change it down the road just in case.<P>thanks<P>mope<P>------------------<BR>=======================<P>-= Silver 86 GT, being restored, and following major things to be done: Mr. Mike's Leather, new steering wheel, paint repair, ebrake repair, take apart and clean everything! <A HREF="http://www.mastergraphics.cjb.net" TARGET=_blank>www.mastergraphics.cjb.net</A>  <A HREF="http://www.mope.cjb.net" TARGET=_blank>www.mope.cjb.net</A> ||||reg||12
Z||000006||theogre||05-08-2001||08:29 PM||||The part you are worried about is the U shaped section that sticks off to one side. That thing get's bent then it won't push in the MC piston as far as it should.||||reg||1
